\chapter{Closures and recursion}
A function retains the lexical environment where it was created. Calls use
that environment rather than the caller's local names. Captured mutable
bindings remain shared cells, so closures can implement private state.

\begin{mullcode}
val makeCounter <- fn(start){
    var n <- start;
    return fn(){ n <- n + 1; return n; };
};
val a <- makeCounter(10);
val b <- makeCounter(100);
G:Assert(a() == 11);
G:Assert(a() == 12);
G:Assert(b() == 101);
val factorial <- fn(n){
    if n <= 1 { return 1; }
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
};
G:Assert(factorial(6) == 720);
\end{mullcode}

The function initializer captures its containing environment, and the
declaration then installs the binding in that environment. This permits the
function body to refer to its own name when called later. The same rule allows
mutually recursive functions declared in one scope before either is invoked.

Captured environments remain live objects: a closure can observe later
assignments and later declarations in its captured scope. Capturing does not
freeze a snapshot. To retain a particular value, initialize a separate
immutable binding and capture that binding.

Recursive calls consume the host-configured \api{maxCallDepth}; tail calls are
not optimized. The default is 128 script calls, and expression nesting has a
separate guard. For large traversals, use iteration or a native library
operation rather than relying on unbounded recursive execution.
